WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.500
Film Courage: So I looked at your IMDB and
is your first credit THE PERFECT NANNY with

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.819
Bruce Boxleitner and Tracy Nelson?

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:07.600
Christine Conradt, Screenwriter: Yeah…I
think that is the first one?

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.190
Film Courage: Okay, great.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.290
Is that the one where you got the job from
the re-write and then the producer wanted

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.350
to hire you?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.560
Christine: No.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.100
I got fired off that project?

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.550
Film Courage: On no!

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.909
Christine: That was with a producer where
I was working as an assistant in development

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:29.609
and then left (he sold the company and I left).

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.789
He invited me to come back and do this rewrite,
which I did and I think I did two drafts and

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:36.891
he hired another writer (which was fine).

00:00:36.891 --> 00:00:38.070
I mean at the time I was sort of sad about
it.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.829
At the time I was sort of sad about it but
it was a great learning experience because…I

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:47.340
didn’t know what I didn’t know.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:54.809
And so now looking back to see where I’ve
grown because I’m now the last writer on.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:57.179
It was good for me to go through that experience.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:01:01.379
I was very grateful that he gave me the opportunity
to do that experience because that ended up

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:02.690
being my first credit.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.970
Film Courage: So the one [job] that you were
fired off of (without giving away too much

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.110
information) but did you see what it was [that
caused it] or was it something that was out

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.110
of your control?

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.000
Christine: No, I just wasn’t a good enough
writer to do what they needed me to do.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.159
So I did improve the script and a lot of what
I put in there ended up in the final cut but

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:28.790
I think I just didn’t know structure well
enough to be able to fix some of the issues

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.780
that they had that they needed me to fix.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.120
And so I fixed the character stuff and then
I think they hired another writer to fix all

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.820
the structural stuff that was wrong with it.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.810
Film Courage: And so at that time did you
see it as structure, like a structural defect

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.810
or no?

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.810
Christine: No.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.180
I couldn’t recognize that at that time.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.180
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.220
So then once you were excellent at structure
you saw “Ahhh!

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.860
That’s what it was.”

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.860
Christine: Right.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.850
And I think also just over time understanding,
getting to know more what they want when they

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.619
tell you we need this or we want this what
that means and how to incorporate that into

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:05.740
a screenplay.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.910
We want to add edge to this, it’s not edgy
enough.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.320
That’s a very obscure idea.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:18.550
So hearing that when I was that early in my
career I don’t think I understood how to

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:23.250
implement edge and now when someone says that
I say okay now I understand the questions

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:28.920
to ask and they can tell me what they really
want and I understand what they mean by that.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.360
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.620
So let’s suppose it’s a housewife that
has everything perfect and nothing is wrong

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.069
how can you incorporate some edge for that
[story]?

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:45.560
Because I am picturing that this is the Lifetime
movie that I knew which is still interesting

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.010
but what edge would we still add to that character?

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:54.450
Christine: Well a lot of times they might
have a dark secret or something in their past

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:59.950
that they feel ashamed of and they’ve kept
from their husband, so something like that

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.760
that is just kind of lurking in the background
that seems to resurface and it doesn’t even

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.760
have to be kind of the A story.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.290
It an just be something that complicates the
A story, right?

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:15.930
So let’s say you’re a housewife, you’re
trying to get pregnant, she’s finally gotten

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:23.870
pregnant and she has this ex-boyfriend who
is getting out of prison and who still wants

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:24.950
to be with her.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.769
And so now he is going to come back and complicate
her life.

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:31.330
And that may not be the A story but it’s
still something that now she has to tell her

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.290
husband who this person is and that kind of
thing.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.290
Film Courage: Right.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.290
And there is the edge.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.900
Christine: And there’s edge and intrigue
to that.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:47.290
So it’s adding to, it’s rounding out that
story and making people real.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:52.260
Because everybody has things that they are
not proud of or whatever and so this is something

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:57.680
that you can add those and we can go even
further in the Lifetime movies or TV movies

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.849
(so that kind of stuff).

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:03.709
Film Courage: So is the aim to show sort of
the perfection and then as we said before

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.049
backing out to show that it’s not perfect?

00:04:07.049 --> 00:04:08.970
Christine: I don’t know if it’s to show
perfect.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:14.300
I think a lot of what intrigues people are
the pretenses, right?

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.370
I think so much of what we try to do on Facebook
is to show everybody in the world this great

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.430
life we have.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.620
You don’t take the picture of the crappy
Ramen noodles you made last night because

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.660
you were so tired to cook.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.130
You take the photo when you’re at the nice
restaurant, right?

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.240
Or this beautiful thing that you made, that
you spent hours on.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.970
We put out a life that we want other people
to see because that’s where we think they

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.530
are forming their opinions of us.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.760
The irony is that most people care more about
themselves than they care about anyone else

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.960
anyway, so they are not even forming those
opinions.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.250
But that’s what we do.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.160
Are characters are the same way.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.250
Our characters live lives based on what they
think they should be living.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:57.970
And so that’s why it’s fun when you can
kind of flush these characters out and give

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:04.010
them these things in their past or things
that they’re doing or temptations or whatever

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.040
that cause those little things of conflict.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.520
Film Courage: And they always have to have
a great place to live.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.860
I mean, I’ve never seen a woman in a Lifetime
movie live in a bad place.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.460
Christine: They live in beautiful houses.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.420
There is definitely an escapism element to
that as well.

